id: pm-lu6sca
title: "A declared provenance resolver that fails at runtime is recorded identically to a dimension the harness cannot supply, so a broken probe is invisible until someone reads the source"
description: "Established by measurement on 2026-08-03 and generalized from a confirmed instance.\n\nToday a provenance dimension resolves to exactly two observable states in the immutable record: a value, or null. Null is overloaded. It is the correct and permanent encoding for all of the following, which have nothing in common:\n\n- the harness genuinely publishes no such dimension (declared via provenance_unavailable_dimensions, an honest absence)\n- no resolver is declared for that dimension on that harness\n- a resolver is declared, ran, and failed - wrong path, unreadable file, unparsable content, revoked permission\n- probes are disabled by settings or PM_AGENT_PROBES\n\nThe confirmed instance: the claude_session_file model resolver computes a session path with an incomplete slug encoding, throws ENOENT, is swallowed by a bare catch, and writes null. The workspace where this happens is this repository. Every mutation it recorded looked exactly like a mutation from a harness that has no model to report. The condition was found by reading the resolver source and recomputing the path by hand, which is not a detection mechanism.\n\nWhy the existing instruments do not cover it. summarizeAgentModelProvenance can report per-harness coverage and can set an inert flag when a declared signal produced only unavailable values, but it reads the history corpus after the fact and it is not wired to anything - that gap has its own owner. Even fully wired, it would report 'coverage is zero' without being able to say whether the cause is an unavailable dimension or a broken probe, because the corpus does not carry that distinction. The distinction has to be recorded at write time; it cannot be recovered later from an immutable record that never held it.\n\nThis is the instrument-integrity pattern applied one level below where it has been applied before. Prior findings established that a gate which never fails is a hypothesis until you name the input that would fail it. The same argument applies to a resolver: a probe that can only be observed succeeding is a probe nobody can prove broken. The fix is not more probing - it is recording the outcome category of the attempt the runtime already makes.\n\nPrivacy boundary: the failure category must be a bounded reason code. Recording the path that failed to open would publish the operator's directory layout into an immutable stream, which the write-time mutation guard exists to prevent.\n\nScope is the outcome vocabulary and its surfacing. Fixing any individual resolver, and pointing the existing corpus analyzers at real data, are separate owners."
type: Feature
status: open
priority: 2
tags[5]: "area:sdk",identity,instrument-integrity,observability,provenance
created_at: "2026-08-03T06:22:12.243Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-03T06:23:55.717Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
acceptance_criteria: "For each declared provenance dimension the runtime distinguishes: resolved, harness-declares-unavailable, resolver-attempted-and-failed, and no-resolver-declared; The failure category records a bounded reason code, never a path or any environment value; pm health surfaces a warning when a declared resolver has a non-zero attempt count and a zero success count in the current workspace; The distinction is observable without reading history: a single bounded command answers 'is my identity provenance working here'; Recording the category costs no additional probe: it is derived from the attempt the runtime already makes"
value: "Turns a silent permanent data loss into a diagnosable condition at the moment it starts, rather than after an immutable corpus has already been written without the field."
outcome: "Resolver health is a first-class, queryable signal: for every declared resolver the workspace can state how many attempts it made, how many resolved, and why the rest did not."
why_now: "A model resolver shipped, failed on its first real workspace, and wrote a full day of null model attributions before anyone noticed - because null is also the correct value when a harness publishes nothing."
parent: pm-5oj5
risk: medium
confidence: high
